wql!: `aggregate`: `sum` over a value of type `u64` — the aggregate accumulates in `i64` and the argument is emitted into that accumulator VERBATIM, with no normalizing conversion — a `u64`/`usize` value is normalized to i64 by the EL, and that cast REINTERPRETS rather than converts: every value at or above 2^63 comes back NEGATIVE, so the number the query goes on to use is not the number the type held — and nothing after this position can tell that number from one the column genuinely held: what leaves here is a plain i64 and every later comparison is a total i64 order, so the query does not fail, it answers something else. The COLUMN is not the problem and is refused nowhere else — ordering by it, grouping by it, comparing it and projecting it all carry `u64` itself; it is this position that must make it an i64 and cannot do so faithfully. The remedy is a value that fits i64 (u32 and narrower widen losslessly), or the i64 it was computed from
